Harbourview Bakery is a fictional bakery near the water in Toronto, baking sourdough, croissants and celebration cakes since 2014. The photos are real bakery photos from Unsplash, credited on the site.

## Problem

A bakery's website has a few simple jobs: make people hungry, tell them what's on the counter today, say when the doors are open, and take cake orders without a phone call. Most bakery owners also want to update it themselves, in the editor they already use, without paying a developer for every price change.

## What it does

- **Photos that make you hungry.** A full-width hero of fresh sourdough, then the three things people come in for: the Country Sourdough, the Butter Croissant and the Cardamom Cinnamon Bun, each linking to its part of the menu.
- **A menu.** Bread, viennoiserie, sweet things and celebration cakes, each with its price.
- **Custom cake orders.** A cake order form with the event date, cake size (from a 6 inch round to two tiers and up) and a description, with the notice needed for celebration and wedding cakes. A Recent Cakes gallery shows what the bakery has made.
- **About.** The bakery's story, from a weekend market stall to a team of eleven, still using the same sourdough starter.
- **Visit Us.** Address, phone, hours for each day, parking and transit notes, and a map. The hours are also in the footer on every page.
- **A blog.** Posts like how to feed a sourdough starter, planning your wedding cake and the holiday order guide.
- **A site-wide banner** for news such as holiday pre-orders.
- **Order ahead.** A spot on the home page and the Custom Cakes page that shows the phone number and cake form today, and takes an online ordering button like the one on [Northwind Coffee](/work/northwind-coffee-ordering) when the bakery is ready.
- **A support chat** button for quick questions, from the [website support bot](/work/docs-chat) project.

## How it helps your business

- **More cake orders.** The form works, is easy to fill in on a phone, and every request lands in the bakery's inbox.
- **Fewer "are you open?" calls,** because the hours are on every page.
- **Found on Google.** Every page has a proper title and description, and the address, hours and menu are marked up so search engines can show them.
- **No new editor to learn.** It's the same WordPress, theme and page builder the owner already uses.

## How it's built

- **WordPress with OceanWP and Elementor,** a small child theme, and a handful of must-use plugins instead of a pile of extras.
- **Fast after a rescue.** The site started out slow, with a broken form and hidden from Google. [How this site was made fast](/work/site-rescue), with before and after Lighthouse numbers, is its own project.
- **Photos sized for the screen.** Every photo is cropped to the shape it's shown in and served as WebP, with smaller versions for phones.
- **Accessible.** Labelled form fields, visible keyboard focus and text colours that pass WCAG AA contrast.
- **Rebuilt from scripts.** The whole site can be recreated from scratch in Docker, so every change is reviewable.

## Stack

WordPress, PHP, the OceanWP theme with Elementor, Contact Form 7 with mail sent through a real mail service, page caching, and Docker for local builds.

## Outcome

A warm, quick bakery website the owner can keep editing herself: browse the menu, start a cake order for a birthday, check Sunday hours and the map, then read about why the croissants take three days. The full story of the speed and repair work is in the [site rescue project](/work/site-rescue).
